  "result": "I wrote the 30-minute lesson on O3, `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`, and a five-item quiz with its key. I haven't checked the lesson's word count or opened it in a browser. I estimate about 3,400 words, inside the 2,700–6,750 range that fits a 30-minute lesson.\n\nThe win: you can say what the makerspace assistant keeps in its context, what it saves between sessions, and how it finds the right page in a long manual.\n\nThe lesson covers:\n- **Context window:** what it holds and why you pay for it on every call.\n- **Between steps:** the program keeps the message list and re-sends it, because the model remembers nothing.\n- **Between sessions:** nothing survives unless you save it.\n- **Notes:** short, factual notes, loaded back at the start of the next session.\n- **Retrieval:** a manual-search tool that brings in only a few chunks.\n\nIt has three figures, four self-checking practice questions, and a worked example that follows one student (Priya) through a session. A faded exercise follows with another student (Sam). The warm-up is a recall question on the agent loop.\n\n**Sources:** I only read search-result summaries of the Anthropic articles, not the full pages, so I kept the lesson's claims close to what those summaries said. It cites Anthropic's context-engineering article and its context-management announcement, and links the Claude Platform cookbook. They are logged in `RESOURCES.md`. I found no vetted online community and left that as a gap. I used your design mentors as the place to test the skill.\n\n**Quiz:** the quiz 0002 is ready when you want it. I haven't shown it or any answers here. Key distractors reuse `M3` (the idea that memory is automatic) from quiz 0001. I'm assuming that is what `M3` means, since the workspace doesn't spell it out.\n\n**Notes:** `NOTES.md` now records that the lesson and quiz exist. I wrote no learning record, since you haven't shown any new understanding yet.\n\nAsk me anything about the lesson in the chat. Do you want a shorter or longer version, or the quiz now?",
